"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[22],{5022:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2016-04-09T15:48:00","modified":"2019-11-12T13:39:00","id":1030,"title":"Element.querySelectorAll might not do what you think it does","article":"<p>When switching from using jQuery, or another selector library, to using the built in <code>document.querySelectorall(&#39;.mySelector&#39;)</code> generally it works the same. You do have to be aware that some libraries give additional selectors that aren&#39;t present in the library such as <code>:not()</code> however.</p>\\n<p>In most of the selector libraries you can scope your selector to a particular root element that is not the document.</p>\\n<h4 id=\\"fig-1\\">Fig 1</h4>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-html\\"><span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">body</span>&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">div</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">id</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"parentElement\\"</span>&gt;</span>\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">div</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">class</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"outerChild\\"</span>&gt;</span>\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">div</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">class</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"innerChild\\"</span>&gt;</span>\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">div</span>&gt;</span>\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">div</span>&gt;</span>\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">div</span>&gt;</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">body</span>&gt;</span></code></pre>\\n<p>Using the html in Fig 1 the example below that shows how in jQuery you would get all elements that are descendants of element, that matches a selector, in this case a div. This would return two elements the outerChild div and the innerChild div.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> parentElement = $(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'#parentElement\'</span>);\\n\\n$(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'div\'</span>, parentElement);\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">//or </span>\\n$(parentElement).find(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'div\'</span>);</code></pre>\\n<p>In the built-in native querySelectorAll we would perform the same process in the example below. We use the querySelectorAll method on the parentElement directly.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> parentElement = <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">document</span>.getElementById(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'parentElement\'</span>);\\n\\nparentElement.querySelectorAll(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'div\'</span>);</code></pre>\\n<p>So far so good, however if you where to change the selector and ask for any div inside of another div things go a little awry. Using jQuery we would do the following which would return a single element, the innerChild.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\">$(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'div div\'</span>, parentElement);\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// or </span>\\n$(parentElement).find(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'div div\'</span>);</code></pre>\\n<p>Using the built-in querySelectorAll we get a different result, in the example below it would return two divs, the innerChild obviously but also the outerChild!</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\">parentElement.querySelectorAll(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'div div\'</span>);</code></pre>\\n<p>The reason for this is when running the querySelectorAll the browser doesnt match from the parentElement as its &#39;root&#39;, where it looks it at the inner html and matches that, it matches your selector against the whole document, then checks the list of what it has found and excludes the elements that are not under your parentElement.</p>\\n<p>As both the outerChild and the innerChild are inside of divs and both are located under the parentElement, it doesn&#39;t matter that the the div surrounding the outerChild is not also a descendant of the parentElement at all. If working with the &#39;component&#39; idea of self controlled pieces of code this can be confusing.</p>\\n<p>Also it means descendant selectors such as &quot;&lt;&quot; don&#39;t work as it will be trying to match it against the document.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"scoped-queryselectorall\\">Scoped-QuerySelectorAll</h2>\\n<p>To combat this, I created a simple wrapper for the element.querySelectorAll called scoped-queryselectorall and is available on <a href=\\"https://github.com/lski/scoped-queryselectorall\\">github</a> but also on bower and <a href=\\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/scoped-queryselectorall\\">npm</a> and is a UMD build so can be used directly in the browser or as a module.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">npm i scoped-queryselectorall --save\\n// or\\nbower install scoped-queryselectorall --save</code></pre>\\n<p>It can be used for both document relative and element relavtive queries:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> qry <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'scoped-queryselectorall\'</span>;\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Document relative</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> elements = qry(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'div div\'</span>);\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Element relative</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> elements = qry(parentElement, <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'div div\'</span>);</code></pre>\\n<p>To achieve the same effect as jQuery it adds a temporary unique <code>data-*</code> attribute to the parent element and pre-appends a selector for that <code>data-*</code> attribute to the original selector to restrict it to inner elements only.</p>\\n<p><strong>Update 2019/11/12</strong> </p>\\n<p>If IE is not an browser you need to support, you can use the <code>:scope</code> selector to achieve the same thing as <code>scoped-queryselectorall</code></p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> elements = parentElement.querySelectorAll(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\':scope div div\'</span>);</code></pre>\\n<p>See <a href=\\"https://caniuse.com/#feat=mdn-css_selectors_scope_dom_api\\">Can I Use</a> for more information.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"references\\">References</h2>\\n<ul>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document/querySelectorAll\\">MDN querySelectorAll</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/:scope\\">MDN :scope</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://caniuse.com/#feat=mdn-css_selectors_scope_dom_api\\">Can I Use</a></li>\\n</ul>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["javascript","selectors"]}')}}]);